<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Republic of Serbian Krajina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1991">1991</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Serbian Krajina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Republic of Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krajina">Krajina</a> was a self-proclaimed Serb state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, declared in 1991 by Croatian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> opposed to Croatian independence and backed by the Yugoslav army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. It covered about a third of Croatia, including the old Military Frontier lands, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knin">Knin</a>, and expelled much of its Croat population. It was overrun by the Croatian army in Operation Storm in August 1995, when most of its Serb inhabitants fled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/republic-of-serbian-krajina.jpg" alt="Republic of Serbian Krajina" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marko Maljkovic and Vladimir Jovanic / Марко Маљковић и Владимир Јовановић · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:State_Flag_of_Serbian_Krajina_(1991).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krajina">Krajina</a>, meaning frontier, came from the Military Frontier that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy carved out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> between 1553 and 1578 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. In the 1991 census <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> made up about 12.2 per cent of the population of Croatia. After Franjo Tuđman was elected president of Croatia in April 1990, the Serb Democratic Party of Jovan Rašković broke with the Croatian parliament. In August 1990 Serbs blocked roads with logs in what became known as the Log Revolution, cutting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> off from the rest of the country, and a referendum confined to Serbs backed Serb sovereignty and autonomy by 99.7 per cent.</p><p>The Croatian constitution of December 1990 reduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> from a constituent people to a national minority. On 21 December 1990 the administration of Milan Babić announced a Serbian Autonomous Oblast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krajina">Krajina</a>, which on 1 April 1991 declared that it would secede from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Wider war began in August 1991. Between August and December approximately 80,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> were expelled and some were killed; estimates of all exiled Croats and other non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> range from 170,000, the figure of the International Criminal Tribunal for the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, to a quarter of a million, that of Human Rights Watch. On 19 December 1991 the autonomous oblasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krajina">Krajina</a> and Western Slavonia proclaimed the republic, and that of Slavonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baranja">Baranja</a> and Western Syrmia was added on 26 February 1992.</p><p>The Vance plan and the Sarajevo Agreement of January 1992 established four United Nations Protected Areas and froze the front lines for three years. Refugees were not allowed to return. Babić, who opposed the plan, was replaced as president on 26 February 1992 by Goran Hadžić; Milan Martić won the presidency in January 1994. The economy depended wholly on the rump <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>: by 1994 only 36,000 of 430,000 citizens were employed. Croatian forces took Western Slavonia in Operation Flash in May 1995.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In Operation Storm between 150,000 and 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> fled, and only 5,000 to 6,000, mostly elderly, remained; some who refused to leave were murdered, tortured or expelled by the Croatian army and police. The remaining territory in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> was placed under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> transitional authority by the Erdut Agreement and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> in 1998.</p><p>The tribunal sentenced Babić, who pleaded guilty, to 13 years in 2004 and Milan Martić to 35 years in 2007. The Croatian generals Ante Gotovina and Mladen Markač were convicted in April 2011 of crimes during Operation Storm and acquitted on appeal in November 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Republic of Serbian Krajina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It left large Serb communities in Croatia, whose Krajina later became the base of the <b>Republic of Serbian Krajina</b> in the 1990s.</blockquote><a href="/state/military-frontier">Military Frontier</a></li></ul></section>
